WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:05.980
These two like to be in perfect union - not
only in their workout routine.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:11.150
Woody Milintachinda and Ote Chabchitrchaidol
are more than just training buddies.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:16.560
The pair fell in love 12 years ago and have
been together ever since.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.540
Three times a week they start their day with
a workout.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.230
They say helps their bodies and their relationship
stay in shape.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:30.750
"Me and my partner are always on the go all
the time working, getting tasks done.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:36.670
I think the only time that we actually get
to spend together known as quality time is

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:41.260
during our morning workouts like today."

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.140
Woody is a well-known TV presenter in Thailand.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.239
For more than a decade he has been in the
public eye.

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:52.159
When he came out 4 years ago it made big headlines
- not all of them kind.

00:00:52.159 --> 00:00:57.929
Woody feels Thailand still has a long way
to go towards full acceptance of sexual minorities.

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:02.350
He welcomes the new bill as an awakening for
Thai society.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:09.890
"People just started talking about this seriously
over the past year.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.979
And everyone wondered: What's a civil partnership?

00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:17.840
Do gays really need to get married?

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.750
The conversations are out there in a society
that has never talked about this."

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.750
Nada Chaiyajit is an LGBT activist.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.469
She acknowledges that a civil union would
bring some improvements for same-sex couples

00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:32.890
- for example when it comes to succession.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.399
At the same time she - like many activists
- fears that the move could entrench their

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:39.679
status as second-class citizens.

00:01:39.679 --> 00:01:50.610
"If the law is discriminated by itself it
is difficult to create a mindset for society

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.740
to understand that we are equal.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.789
So people will look at us and say 'Oh, you
are second class citizens because you cannot

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:07.580
adopt children, you cannot enjoy social welfare
which is a huge move from my point of view.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:15.420
So instead of taking baby steps to take some
rights, I would go for full rights."

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.510
Woody and Ote take a different view.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.800
They feel Thailand isn't ready for full marriage
equality.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.150
Demanding all might just result in achieving
nothing.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:31.590
"If you push too hard you might be aggravating
the people that are living here.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:36.120
So it is about knocking at the doors, opening
the doors one by one."

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.190
"It's a first step, a very important first
step.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:45.940
And if we manage to get this first step right
we will still be the very first in Southeast

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.330
Asia to get that.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:56.230
And I think that on its own is such an achievement
both for Thailand, for the region, it's something

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.120
we can be proud of."

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.160
Most of the heavy lifting is done.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.650
What's left now is the vote in parliament.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:07.090
Should the civil union bill pass Woody and
Ote want to be among the first same-sex couples

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:09.400
in Thailand to legally tie the knot.

